<section>
  <hgroup>
    <h1>About EventGlimpse</h1>
    <p>Simple photo sharing built for real events</p>
  </hgroup>

  <article>
    <h3>The Problem</h3>
    <p>
      At weddings, parties, and family gatherings, everyone takes photos on their phones. 
      But getting those photos shared? Too much friction. Group chats get messy. 
      Email attachments are awkward. Cloud storage requires logins. Photos stay trapped on phones.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article>
    <h3>The Solution</h3>
    <p>
      EventGlimpse removes the friction. Create a gallery, share one link (or QR code), 
      and guests upload photos instantly - no account required. That's it.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>One link per event</strong> - easy to share, easy to remember</li>
      <li><strong>No guest accounts</strong> - anyone with the link can upload</li>
      <li><strong>QR codes built-in</strong> - print and display at your event</li>
      <li><strong>No app install</strong> - works in any phone browser</li>
      <li><strong>All photos in one place</strong> - no hunting across platforms</li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article>
    <h3>Why not just use Facebook/Google Photos/iCloud?</h3>
    <p>You could! But here's what usually happens:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Facebook</strong> - Not everyone has it, albums get buried in feeds, photo quality gets compressed</li>
      <li><strong>Google Photos</strong> - Requires Google accounts, sharing settings are confusing, "shared albums" get lost</li>
      <li><strong>iCloud</strong> - Only works well if everyone has iPhones</li>
      <li><strong>Group chats</strong> - Photos get mixed with messages, impossible to find later</li>
      <li><strong>AirDrop</strong> - Only works nearby, iPhone-only, chaotic at events</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      EventGlimpse works for <em>everyone</em> at the event - any phone, no accounts, no apps. 
      Just scan and upload. That's the difference.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article>
    <h3>Built for Real Events</h3>
    <p>
      EventGlimpse was created after experiencing the frustration of losing event photos 
      to the "I'll send them later" promise. We tested it at real events and refined it 
      based on one simple metric: how many people upload without being asked twice?
    </p>
    <p>
      No complex features. No social networking. Just a friction-free way to collect 
      photos from the people who were there.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article>
    <h3>Privacy & Data</h3>
    <p>
      Your photos are stored securely in AWS S3. Gallery links are private (not searchable). 
      Only people with the link can view or upload. Gallery creators can delete photos 
      or entire galleries at any time.
    </p>
  </article>

  <div class="grid">
    <% if (user) { %>
      <a href="/galleries" role="button">My Galleries</a>
    <% } else { %>
      <a href="/register" role="button">Get Started</a>
      <a href="/" role="button" class="outline">Back to Home</a>
    <% } %>
  </div>
</section>
